<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 1: Introduction                                           -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-intro-responsible-ai" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Introduction: AI in Kenya</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-intro-responsible-ai">
        <p>
            AI is already being used in Kenya, in clinics, on farms, in banks
            and in classrooms.
            Most of what you read about AI comes from the West, and it's easy
            to forget that people here are using it too, on problems they
            actually have.
        </p>

        <p>
            This book is about using it well.
            What would it mean to use AI <em>responsibly</em> in Kenya?
            Who needs to be involved: the people who build it, buy it and
            regulate it, and the people whose lives it ends up affecting?
            We start this chapter with a story, about loans.
            Then we step back and ask what <q>responsible AI</q> actually
            means, which turns out to be harder to pin down than you'd think.
        </p>

        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Describe how AI is used in Kenya today, using digital
                        lending as a detailed example.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain, with an example, how the same technology can
                        both help people and put them at risk.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Say why <q>responsible AI</q> is more than a label, and
                        give a working description of it.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain what <q>humans in the loop</q> means and why it
                        matters.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Tell the difference between the hype around AI and the
                        advances that will last.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 1. Case study                                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-intro-responsible-ai-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: A Loan Decided by Your Phone</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-lending-problem">
            <title>The problem: credit without collateral</title>

            <p>
                If you want a loan from a bank, they usually want some kind of
                security first.
                That might be a title deed, a payslip, or someone to guarantee
                the loan.
                Lots of people in Kenya don't have any of those.
                What many of them do have is a phone, and many of them use
                M-PESA.
            </p>

            <p>
                M-PESA is a mobile money service run by Safaricom, and it
                launched in 2007.
                You can send money, receive it, keep it and pay bills using
                just your phone, with no bank account needed.
            </p>

            <p>
                Digital lenders saw the gap.
                Instead of asking for collateral, they use machine learning to
                predict whether you'll pay a loan back, based on whatever they
                can find out about you
                (<xref ref="biblio-policy-brief-2022" text="custom">AI Utilization in Kenya, 2022</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Tala is one of the best-known.
                It says it began lending to people in Kenya in 2014.
                Its founder, Shivani Siroya, had spent time interviewing people
                in emerging markets, and decided that the main thing stopping
                them getting loans was a lack of data
                (<xref ref="biblio-tala-faq" text="custom">Tala, n.d.</xref>).
                So Tala built an app that turns what's on your phone into a
                credit score.
            </p>

            <p>
                According to Tala, its loans range from $10 to $500 (about KES
                1,300 to KES 65,000), they last 21 to 90 days, and you get an
                offer within minutes.
                It also says it has lent over $3.4 billion (about KES 442
                billion) to nearly 8 million customers, across all the
                countries it works in and not only Kenya
                (<xref ref="biblio-tala-faq" text="custom">Tala, n.d.</xref>).
                Those are the company's own figures (and the page they come
                from looks a few years old, so they may be out of date and the
                real numbers might be higher), and we've converted the dollar
                amounts at about KES 130 to the dollar, which is the 2026 rate.
            </p>

            <p>
                If you have no payslip and no history with a bank, that's a
                real option.
                You can pay school fees, or cover an emergency.
                That's the good part, and it matters.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-lending-how">
            <title>How the decision is made</title>

            <p>
                To decide, the app needs data about you.
                Tala says its models use machine learning on data you choose
                to share from your phone, and that they look for patterns that
                show you're consistent
                (<xref ref="biblio-tala-faq" text="custom">Tala, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The examples Tala gives are interesting.
                If you use lots of apps and lots of data, it takes that as a
                sign you can afford data charges, so it reads that as a sign
                you can afford to repay.
                If you fill in the application thoroughly, it takes that as a
                sign you're serious about the loan
                (<xref ref="biblio-tala-faq" text="custom">Tala, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Neither of those directly measures whether you'll repay.
                They're guesses, based on behaviour that happened to line up
                with repayment in the data the model learned from.
                Think about what that means for someone with an old phone and a
                small data bundle.
            </p>

            <p>
                Tala's privacy policy lists the data it can collect once you
                give permission.
                That includes identifiers for your phone, your location, your
                contacts and keywords from your SMS messages.
                It says an automated system then works out how much you can
                borrow
                (<xref ref="biblio-tala-privacy" text="custom">Tala Kenya, n.d.</xref>).
                Tala also says that it doesn't read what your messages say, but
                it does collect details about them, such as the time, the phone
                number and keywords
                (<xref ref="biblio-tala-2022" text="custom">Tala Kenya, 2022</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Tala isn't the only lender, and other apps may collect more.
                The 2022 policy brief says lending apps use data such as
                contacts, SMS messages, what's on the SD card, the gallery and
                the apps you've downloaded
                (<xref ref="biblio-policy-brief-2022" text="custom">AI Utilization in Kenya, 2022</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                In 2021, CIPIT looked at seven popular lending apps in Kenya,
                Tala and Branch among them, looking at their policies and
                practices in late 2020.
                They found that the apps asked for a lot of permissions.
                Some had trackers that profile what borrowers do, and some
                shared data with third parties for marketing and advertising.
                None had published a data protection impact assessment.
                CIPIT's conclusion was that the apps' policies and practices
                didn't comply with the Data Protection Act
                (<xref ref="biblio-cipit-2021" text="custom">CIPIT, 2021</xref>).
                That's a small sample from a few years ago, and the report
                doesn't say that every app did all of this.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-lending-harm">
            <title>Where things can go wrong</title>

            <p>
                Let's step back and look at what has happened here.
                Someone's phone, with their contacts, messages and location on
                it, has been used to make a decision about their money.
                That raises some questions.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Consent.</alert>
                        Did they understand what they were agreeing to, and did
                        they have a real choice?
                        Tala's privacy policy says that by accepting it, you
                        confirm that automated processing is needed for the
                        service and you consent to it
                        (<xref ref="biblio-tala-privacy" text="custom">Tala Kenya, n.d.</xref>).
                        But ticking a box on a small screen isn't the same as
                        understanding.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>People who never agreed.</alert>
                        Your contacts didn't agree to anything.
                        Privacy International reports that at least seven
                        lending apps in Kenya have texted or even called the
                        contacts of people who had missed repayments, to put
                        pressure on them
                        (<xref ref="biblio-pi-fintech" text="custom">Privacy International, 2021</xref>).
                        Tala says that it will never call your contacts
                        (<xref ref="biblio-tala-2022" text="custom">Tala Kenya, 2022</xref>).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Explanation and appeal.</alert>
                        If the app turns you down, or offers a small amount, can
                        you find out why, or challenge it?
                        Kenya's Data Protection Act, 2019 says you have a right
                        not to be subject to a decision based only on automated
                        processing, if it significantly affects you.
                        There are exceptions, including where the decision is
                        needed for a contract or you've consented.
                        If such a decision is made, you must be told in writing,
                        and you can ask for it to be reconsidered, or for a new
                        decision that isn't only automated
                        (<xref ref="biblio-dpa-2019" text="custom">Data Protection Act, 2019</xref>, section 35).
                        Tala's policy sets out this right, and also says it
                        can't guarantee that a reconsidered decision will change
                        (<xref ref="biblio-tala-privacy" text="custom">Tala Kenya, n.d.</xref>).
                        Whether the right works in practice for a borrower on a
                        phone is a different question.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Fairness.</alert>
                        Does the model work equally well for everyone?
                        Or does it get it wrong for people whose phone use looks
                        different from the people it learned from?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                Some of this has already been tested.
                On 26 September 2023, the Office of the Data Protection
                Commissioner (ODPC) issued a penalty notice of KES 2.975
                million to Mulla Pride Ltd, which
                runs the KeCredit and Faircash lending apps.
                The ODPC found that it had used people's names and contact
                details, which it got from third parties, to send threatening
                messages and make threatening phone calls
                (<xref ref="biblio-odpc-2023" text="custom">ODPC, 2023</xref>).
                TechCabal also reported that the company wasn't on the
                Central Bank of Kenya's list of approved lenders, and that the
                most the law allowed at the time was a fine of KES 5 million
                (<xref ref="biblio-techcabal-fines" text="custom">Abuya, 2023</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-lending-response">
            <title>What happened next</title>

            <p>
                The Central Bank of Kenya now licenses digital lenders, and
                companies like Tala say they're licensed
                (<xref ref="biblio-tala-licence" text="custom">Tala Kenya, 2023</xref>).
                The data protection regulator is also holding lenders to the
                Data Protection Act.
                TechCabal's view is that online lenders went largely
                unregulated for years while some loan apps harassed borrowers,
                so the rules came after the harm
                (<xref ref="biblio-techcabal-fines" text="custom">Abuya, 2023</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-lending-lessons">
            <title>What the case shows</title>

            <p>
                Digital lending gave loans to people the banks weren't serving.
                It also put people's privacy at risk.
                At some lenders, it was used to put pressure on borrowers'
                contacts, and automated decisions can be hard to question.
                Both of those are true, and one doesn't cancel out the other.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    Much of this isn't a bug in the code.
                    These systems did what they were built to do.
                    What we're asking is whether it was built and used
                    <em>responsibly</em>, and that's about people, data and
                    accountability as much as it's about the algorithm.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                The same questions apply well beyond lending.
                Who checks what a farming chatbot tells a farmer?
                Who does a patient go to when a health tool gets it wrong?
                The exercises at the end of the chapter give you three more
                cases to think about.
                AI isn't a silver bullet, and the conversation about using it
                responsibly needs to happen while it's being adopted, not
                afterwards.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 3. What do we mean by responsible AI?                          -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-intro-responsible-ai-meaning">
        <title>What Do We Mean by Responsible AI?</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                You've probably already worried about AI.
                Maybe it's the environment, or what happens to people's data,
                or whether it's fair.
                And if you suggest using AI for something, someone will soon
                ask how you're going to do that ethically, or responsibly.
            </p>

            <p>
                This section is about that question.
                The ideas come from a conversation between David Stern and Kate
                Fleming, two directors of IDEMS, on the IDEMS Podcast
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-204" text="custom">Stern and Fleming, 2025</xref>).
                They don't claim to have the answers, and neither do we.
            </p>

            <p>
                You can listen to the episode here, and it's about forty
                minutes long.
                You don't need to listen to it before reading on, but it's
                worth a listen at some point.
                It's also available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/204-what-does-responsible-ai-really-mean/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-intro-responsible-ai-podcast">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 204: What does responsible AI really mean?</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-intro-responsible-ai-podcast"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/8311/204-what-does-responsible-ai-really-mean.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-define">
            <title>A label isn't an answer</title>

            <p>
                <q>Responsible AI</q> can sound like a phrase organisations
                stick on whatever they were already doing.
                Saying <q>trust me, it's responsible</q> is easy.
                Explaining what you mean is much harder.
            </p>

            <p>
                Part of the difficulty is where the technology came from.
                Kate points out that the large language models behind most AI
                tools today come from a small number of big private companies,
                with very specific goals of their own.
                She says that some of how they were built is arguably unethical,
                or even criminal, with copyright law as one example.
                So anyone using these tools inherits questions they didn't
                create.
            </p>

            <p>
                The other part is that the technology is young.
                One way to put it is that expecting today's AI to be fully
                responsible is like expecting a toddler to take full
                responsibility.
                It can't yet, so the responsibility has to sit with the adults
                around it.
                That doesn't mean we should avoid it.
                Whether AI ends up helping society depends on the choices
                people make about how it's built, tested and used.
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-intro-responsible-ai-working">
                <title>Responsible AI (a working description)</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <term>Responsible AI</term> means developing and using AI
                        in a way that serves the people it affects, keeps
                        humans accountable for what it does, and takes seriously
                        its costs to people, communities and the environment.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <p>
                That's a description, not a finished definition.
                Responsible AI isn't a settled category.
                It's still being worked out, and that includes communities who
                will need to help set the standards and decide how their data
                is used.
                Each chapter of this book fills in a bit more.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-hype">
            <title>Hype, boom and bust</title>

            <p>
                This isn't the first time people have got excited about AI.
                David's account is that there have been three or four boom
                periods before, and each time public attention dropped away
                when the technology did less than people hoped.
                One he mentions is when a computer beat the world chess
                champion.
            </p>

            <p>
                Each boom starts with a real advance, and this one does too.
                This time it's generative AI, and large language models in
                particular.
                They can write text that's so close to human writing it can be
                hard to tell the difference.
                That's useful, and it's already changing how people write and
                talk.
            </p>

            <p>
                Once a boom gets going, though, most of what follows is noise:
                money, announcements and competition, built on one idea that
                hasn't changed much in years.
                Kate points out that AI companies have a reason to let
                grandiose claims, even scary ones, go unchallenged, because they
                make the technology sound more advanced.
                We should take real risks seriously without believing every
                claim.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    A good question to ask of any AI claim is: <em>what is the
                    specific advance underneath this, and what will still be
                    useful once the excitement has gone?</em>
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                In earlier booms, what survived was substantial and mostly
                good, but you mostly didn't notice it, because it had become
                part of ordinary systems.
                The hope is that the same happens here, and that the lasting
                value is in specific, well-understood uses and not in one
                general machine that does everything.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-costs">
            <title>The costs behind the screen</title>

            <p>
                It's easy to think of AI as the chat window on your phone.
                Behind it there's a lot more: people, energy, water, land and
                data.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Computing power.</alert>
                        Building the next, slightly better model needs a huge
                        amount of computing.
                        David says that a single data centre can use as much power as
                        a small town, and he's worried that demand from data centres is
                        pushing against the shift to clean energy.
                        He also says that a large AI data centre is being built in
                        Zambia.
                        It's fair to ask what's being taken, who benefits and who
                        pays.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>People and data.</alert>
                        Models learn from data that people created, and building
                        them depends on human work that mostly happens out of
                        sight.
                        A later chapter returns to this, using the story of
                        Kenyan workers who labelled harmful content to make
                        chatbots safer.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                Kate points out that governments often feel pressure to rush,
                because they're told that regulation will hold back progress
                and leave them behind.
                One of the aims of this book is to show that there are
                alternatives.
                If a huge amount of money and resource is going into one
                narrow part of society, it's reasonable to ask for more of it
                to go into checking the environmental and social
                consequences.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-humans">
            <title>Humans in the loop</title>

            <definition xml:id="def-intro-responsible-ai-hitl">
                <title>Human in the loop</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        An AI system has a <term>human in the loop</term> when
                        people are deliberately involved in how it is
                        developed, tested and used, so that its outputs are
                        checked, corrected or approved by a person rather than
                        acted on automatically.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <p>
                Lots of principles get suggested for responsible AI, such as
                transparency.
                David says that keeping humans in the loop stands out because the
                others relate to it.
                Even if you can't see inside a model, and have to treat it as a
                black box, having people in the right places means you can
                still see what goes in and what comes out, and you can be open
                about what the system is doing.
            </p>

            <p>
                It matters most where the stakes are high.
                David mentions AI systems that have reportedly encouraged
                suicide, and there's a risk of systems being used to take
                advantage of people who are vulnerable.
                Safeguards need to be designed in, and some people are arguing
                that they should be written into regulation.
            </p>

            <p>
                Think back to the lending apps.
                Who can a borrower ask when a loan is refused?
                Who checks what a farming chatbot tells a farmer?
                Those are questions about where the humans are.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-slm">
            <title>Doing more with less</title>

            <p>
                Large language models do better the more computing power
                they're given, which is why the race for bigger infrastructure
                carries on.
                But most people don't need a model that can do everything.
            </p>

            <p>
                Take the idea of putting the equivalent of a good agricultural
                extension agent in a farmer's pocket: an AI assistant that
                talks to farmers in their own language and gives sound advice.
                David's example is Digital Green, which is trying to have AI
                agents talk with farmers in their own language and give the
                equivalent of good extension advice
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-204" text="custom">Stern and Fleming, 2025</xref>).
                Right now, building something like this means starting from a
                large language model.
                But the task is narrow, and once the system has been built and
                tested, the farmer gets nothing extra from all that power
                behind it.
                So you can ask how much less you'd need to do the same job.
            </p>

            <p>
                This is where <term>small language models</term> come in.
                They're smaller models, built or trimmed for one purpose, that
                use far less computing power and energy.
                The narrower the problem, the less computing you need, and the
                relationship is far from linear.
                David's estimate is that the research needed to make small
                models mature is still going and will take at least five
                years, but choices made now can aim at it.
                That's his expectation, not a settled fact.
            </p>

            <p>
                For Kenya this matters.
                Cheaper models are easier to run locally, in local languages,
                on modest hardware, for communities who can't pay for a global
                cloud.
                They also make it more realistic for local organisations to
                control the technology instead of renting it.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-access">
            <title>Who is AI for?</title>

            <p>
                Kate says that in wealthy places, a lot of AI is used for
                surveillance and productivity, and as an excuse to lay people
                off.
                In low-resource settings it can look different, because the
                scarce thing is often skilled people.
            </p>

            <p>
                For a long time, building technology was gate-kept by people
                with deep technical skills, who could do things others thought
                of as magic, and who tended to build systems that worked for
                people like themselves.
                AI could lower that barrier, so that someone who isn't a
                programmer can build or adapt something that works in their
                own local context.
                That isn't only good news for non-specialists, though.
                The same tools can do some of what specialists do, and nobody's
                skills are guaranteed to be safe.
            </p>

            <p>
                It also doesn't follow that AI means less work.
                There's plenty that needs doing, in health care and education
                for example.
                What's limited is what we choose to pay for and prioritise.
                The aim should be to use technology to improve those jobs, not
                to remove people from them.
            </p>

            <p>
                We'll use the word <term>sociotechnical</term> for this way of
                thinking.
                A system isn't just the software.
                It's the software together with the people, communities, rules
                and incentives around it, and designing AI well means
                designing all of that together.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-uncertainty">
            <title>Working with uncertainty</title>

            <p>
                Nobody knows exactly how all this will turn out, including the
                people who know the most about it.
                That can sound like a reason to avoid the subject.
                A better response is to get comfortable acting under
                uncertainty without gambling.
            </p>

            <p>
                Big bets, where you win or lose and then move on, are common in
                technology and in funding.
                But here it's people's lives and livelihoods at stake, and the
                aim should be stability.
                That points to small, constrained, real-world uses, with
                communities involved, learning from each one and linking them
                together as understanding grows.
                Progress comes in small steps, not in one leap to a utopia,
                which is always somebody else's dystopia.
            </p>

            <p>
                That kind of optimistic realism runs through the book, and it's
                why we started with Kenya's own examples.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        AI is already used in Kenya, often through the phones
                        and platforms people already use.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The same systems that help can also expose people to
                        harm, as the digital lending case study shows.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <q>Responsible AI</q> isn't a settled category.
                        It's still being worked out, and it involves people,
                        data, accountability and environmental cost, as well as
                        algorithms.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Hype comes in cycles.
                        Look for the real advance underneath, and what will
                        still be useful after the noise.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Keeping humans in the loop is a practical starting point
                        for responsibility.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Narrow, specific uses, supported by smaller models,
                        offer a way to do more with less.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 4. Illustrative example using software                         -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-intro-responsible-ai-worked-example">
        <title>Try It: Questioning an AI Assistant</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                You don't need any programming for this one.
                You do need a free AI chat assistant, and about thirty
                minutes.
                We're not trying to work out which assistant is best.
                We just want you to practise noticing what it does, so you can
                ask the questions from this chapter about a real system.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-activity-setup">
            <title>The task</title>

            <p>
                Imagine you're a smallholder potato farmer, and you've noticed
                dark patches on the leaves of your plants.
                You're going to ask an AI assistant for help.
            </p>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Ask in English.
                        Describe the leaves, and ask what could be wrong and
                        what you should do.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Ask the same question in Kiswahili, or another language
                        you speak.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Ask something that depends on where you are, like the
                        best time to plant potatoes in your county.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Ask a follow-up question that you already know the
                        answer to, and see whether the assistant gets it right.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-activity-record">
            <title>What to notice</title>

            <p>
                Use <xref ref="tbl-intro-responsible-ai-notice"/> to guide what
                you write down.
                Put it in your own words, and keep the exact replies so you can
                compare them with someone else's.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="tbl-intro-responsible-ai-notice">
                <title>Observations to record</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>Question</cell>
                        <cell>What to look for</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Was the advice right for Kenya?</cell>
                        <cell>Does it mention crops, seasons, products or
                        conditions that apply here, or is it generic?</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Did the language change the answer?</cell>
                        <cell>Is the Kiswahili reply as detailed, accurate and
                        natural as the English one?</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Did it say what it didn't know?</cell>
                        <cell>Does it admit it's unsure, or ask where you are?
                        Or does it sound confident whatever you ask?</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Could you check it?</cell>
                        <cell>Does it say where the advice comes from?
                        Who could you ask to confirm it?</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>What if it's wrong?</cell>
                        <cell>If you acted on it, who would pay for the
                        mistake, and how much would it cost?</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Where's the human?</cell>
                        <cell>At what point could an extension officer, an
                        agronomist or a neighbour step in?</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-intro-responsible-ai-activity-reflect">
            <title>Reflecting</title>

            <p>
                Now link your notes back to the chapter.
                Which of your observations are about how good the technology
                is, and which are about how it's used and checked?
                A small model, trained for this one task and tested with
                farmers, might improve some of what you saw.
                Which parts, and which parts wouldn't it help with?
                And where would you want a human in the loop before this tool
                was offered to a thousand farmers?
            </p>

            <note>
                <p>
                    Please don't put anyone's personal or financial details
                    into an AI assistant for this activity.
                    Free tools may keep what you type.
                </p>
            </note>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 5. Exercises                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-intro-responsible-ai">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-1">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Pick an AI application that's used in Kenya, either from
                    this chapter or one you know about.
                    Write a short profile of it, answering these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>Who built it, and who pays for it?</li>
                        <li>Who uses it, and who else is affected by it?</li>
                        <li>What data does it use, and where does the data come from?</li>
                        <li>What decision or advice does it give?</li>
                        <li>What happens if it's wrong, and who pays for that?</li>
                        <li>Where is the human in the loop?</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-case-prompts">
            <title>Case: PROMPTS and the nurses</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    PROMPTS is run by Jacaranda Health, a Kenyan non-profit.
                    It began as appointment reminders sent by SMS.
                    Then mothers started texting back, and it grew into a
                    service that uses AI to understand their questions and
                    flag the ones that might be urgent.
                    About 7% of messages get flagged, and those go straight to
                    a nurse.
                    There's a team of 18 nurses who monitor and answer
                    messages around the clock
                    (<xref ref="biblio-npr-2026" text="custom">NPR, 2026</xref>).
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Who is the human in the loop here, and what do they
                            actually do?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            The AI sorts messages into urgent and not urgent.
                            Which is the worse mistake: calling an urgent
                            message not urgent, or the other way round?
                            How should that affect how the system is set up?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Say the nurses get overwhelmed.
                            What pressure might there be to let the AI handle
                            more messages on its own, and what would you want
                            in place to resist it?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            The messages are about pregnancy and health.
                            Who should be able to see them, and how long should
                            they be kept?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            How would you test whether it works just as well
                            for messages written in different languages,
                            spellings or styles?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-case-chatcare">
            <title>Case: Chat Care</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Chat Care is a free AI chatbot from the Kenya Red Cross and
                    Pathways Technologies.
                    It offers mental health support at any time of day, in
                    English and Swahili, and says that all conversations are
                    kept confidential
                    (<xref ref="biblio-redcross" text="custom">Kenya Red Cross, n.d.</xref>).
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            It describes itself as confidential.
                            What would you want to know before you believed
                            that, and who would you ask?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Say a user tells the chatbot they're thinking of
                            harming themselves.
                            What should happen next, and who should be
                            responsible for making sure it does?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            If a chatbot reaches people who'd otherwise get no
                            help at all, should it be held to a lower standard
                            than a human counsellor, a higher one, or the same?
                            Make the case for your answer.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Where could you put a human in the loop, and what
                            would it cost?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-case-farmerai">
            <title>Case: FarmerAI</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Safaricom and Opportunity International launched FarmerAI
                    in February 2025.
                    The pilot ran until August 2025, and aimed to reach up to
                    1,000 potato farmers by SMS and WhatsApp with advice on
                    weather, fertiliser and pest management.
                    The plan was to scale it up later
                    (<xref ref="biblio-safaricom-farmerai" text="custom">Safaricom, 2025</xref>).
                    We couldn't find any published results from the pilot.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What would you need to see published before you
                            believed the pilot had worked?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            A farmer follows the advice and the crop fails.
                            Who pays for that, and is anyone accountable?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who checks what the chatbot tells farmers?
                            If it gave bad advice, how quickly could that be
                            corrected for everyone who got it?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            The service learns where farmers are and what they
                            grow.
                            Who benefits from that data, apart from the farmer?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What should happen to farmers who've come to rely on
                            it if the pilot ends?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-2">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A digital lending app asks to see your contacts, your SMS
                    messages and your photo gallery.
                    For each one, give a reason it could be relevant to whether
                    you'll repay a loan, and a reason it's an intrusion.
                    What would you want the app to tell you before you agree?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-7">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Tala says it treats the number of apps you use, and how
                    thoroughly you fill in the application, as signs of whether
                    you'll repay.
                    Think of two groups of people who might be treated unfairly
                    because of this, even though they'd repay just fine.
                    What would you change?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-3">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a news story or an advert that makes a big claim about
                    AI.
                    What's the specific advance, if any, that the claim depends
                    on?
                    What evidence does it give, and who gains if people believe
                    it?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-4">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A chatbot gives farmers advice on fertiliser and pest
                    control.
                    Design a simple process that keeps humans in the loop.
                    Say who reviews what, how often, and what happens when the
                    reviewer disagrees with the chatbot.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-5">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In your own words, why does a narrower task usually need
                    less computing power?
                    Give an example from your own work or studies of a task
                    where a small model might be enough, and one where it
                    might not.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-intro-responsible-ai-6">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    We compared today's AI to a toddler.
                    What does that help you understand, and where does the
                    comparison stop working?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

